WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:11.199
Now that we've gone over how to communicate from some kind of player or user input into Pawn, some kind of character in the world.

2
00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:19.699
Let's talk about how we actually communicate with that character and the world now, so to do this we talk about blueprint interfaces.

3
00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:29.329
So this is just an overview of the blueprint interface, we'll get hands-on inside of an engine with a quick tutorial on how to use them.

4
00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:35.829
But to give you the high level information right now, so we have two different blueprint interfaces here.

5
00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:42.329
We have a use interface where the player directly uses an actor in the level.

6
00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:51.329
So the player presses a button, they want something to happen, it sends out a message that says I want to use something, is there anything to use here?

7
00:00:51.829 --> 00:01:00.329
And then there's another blueprint interface that can receive an activation message that says I have been used, do something here.

8
00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:11.329
So one interface sends out a message that says I want to use something, and then another interface says I have been used, do X action.

9
00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:22.829
And again, the high level concept is that we're sending out a message to be received from one blueprint to another blueprint and we're just checking if another blueprint can receive this message.

10
00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:25.329
That's the easiest way to think about that concept.

11
00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:31.829
When we are sending out these use object messages, they come in the form of an event.

12
00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:40.329
And you'll see this little icon here which looks like a little blueprint with a cog and some arrows, that is the global icon for blueprint interface.

13
00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:43.829
So whenever you see that, that's what a blueprint interface is.

14
00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:56.829
And so when we're talking about using them, a really good practice to get into whenever you have variables that could be any number of actors, actor types, shall we say, you want to check if it's valid.

15
00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:02.329
You want to say, is this variable valid? Does it have some kind of property that can be used?

16
00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:06.829
So it's really good to prevent errors, check the references early.

17
00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:11.419
Later down the line, they cause compile or even crash issues.

18
00:02:11.419 --> 00:02:19.419
So getting into the habit of using an is valid is a really useful workflow and you don't just need to use it with blueprint interfaces either.

19
00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:23.419
They can be across the board, just getting into using is valid.

20
00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:32.419
So we can drag off the blue pin or whichever kind of pin you have as a variable and then you can type is valid and you'll get this kind of function

21
00:02:32.419 --> 00:02:38.919
that only executes if this variable here has some kind of information that can be used.

22
00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:47.780
Another way to get is valid is you can right click on this blue pin of your variable here and you can convert it to a get is valid.

23
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:54.780
And that will give you this kind of box. So it's still the same variable, it still has an activatable target, which is the same here.

24
00:02:54.780 --> 00:03:02.280
But now we have two execution pins, which are the same execution pins as the top image, but it's just a little bit cleaner way of working.

25
00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.780
So both will do the exact same thing and then that's up to you how you decide to use it in your workflow.

26
00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:13.780
Then the last thing to cover is a does implement interface.

27
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:22.280
So when we're using these interfaces, when maybe we have an activatable target and we have this object here,

28
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:29.280
and then whatever interface we've created, we could select that interface from this drop down here.

29
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.780
And we want to say, does this thing that we're looking at, say it's a light switch in this instance,

30
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:41.780
does it implement the use interface class that we created? And if it does, okay, cool.

31
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:49.280
We'll continue and fire continue the execution of whichever logic we have.

32
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:56.870
So again, we're just kind of securely checking that everything is correct before we fire certain functionality

33
00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:02.370
to make sure the thing that we're trying to interact with is of the right class.
